    "rank": 1776958135.0,
    "content": "Avis Stock Craters 40% Thursday After Historic Squeeze Unwinds - Moby\nAvis Budget Group closed down 38% Wednesday and was down another 40% in morning trading Thursday, last seen around $264 and roughly $600 below where it was 24 hours ago. Who could have seen this coming?\nBut it’s maybe even more chaotic than we thought it would be. Options volume hit 200,000 contracts on Wednesday with implied volatility at 235%, compared to 20% in the S&P 500, and over 10 million shares changed hands in two days against a float so thin that the whole thing stopped being a finance story and became a physics problem. By Wednesday afternoon, brokers were reportedly discussing pushing margin requirements on CAR to 100%, which is how you know the adults have seen enough.\nUnder normal Regulation T rules, you can borrow up to 50 cents on every dollar you invest, buying twice the stock with half the cash. A 100% requirement strips that entirely. Brokers don't make that call because they're feeling cautious. They make it when a stock is so volatile and so untethered from fundamentals that the collateral backing the loan could evaporate faster than they can issue a margin call. It's the repo man showing up before you've missed a payment. Not to take anything, yet, just to let you know he has the address. You can still buy Avis at $264. You just have to do it with money you actually own. The practical effect removes the fuel. No borrowed firepower, no levering up into the squeeze, and suddenly the only buyers left are the ones with real conviction, or real problems, but real cash either way.\nOur analysts just identified a stock with the potential to be the next Nvidia. Tell us how you invest and we'll show you why it's our #1 pick.\nTap here.\nThe GameStop comparison writes itself, and also teaches a lesson that apparently requires periodic re-teaching. In January 2021, Melvin Capital needed a bailout, Robinhood halted buying at the worst possible moment, and the retail army that stormed the castle won the battle and lost the war. The people who bought GME at $400 on the way up held diamond hands straight through the collapse, while the hedge funds that survived the initial squeeze restructured and waited. What GameStop actually proved wasn't that retail could beat Wall Street. It proved that in a squeeze, timing is everything and narrative is nothing. CAR gave the bulls a slightly more defensible story: tariff tailwinds, a thin float, CEO Brian Choi's $800 million to $1 billion EBITDA guidance.\nA meme with footnotes is still a meme. It just feels worse when it unwinds.\nOne stock. Nvidia-level potential. 30M+ investors trust Moby to find it first. Get the pick.\nTap here.",
